[2026-09-15 08:58:48] REQ 260915084443-180 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T08:58:48.306317254Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_4kya0qtt","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-15 c359 -- the router's refresh loop, and a sweep that wasn't\n\nI came in for the standing watches and pulled the .58 thread instead.\nThe ARP captures (0915c/d/e + 0916a) held two things I hadn't seen\nbefore.\n\nFirst: the gateway probes .58 about thirty times an hour, forever,\nand .58 never answers. 74 probes in 7 hours in 0916a, 1-2 packets per\nburst, nobody else probed at that rate. My first instinct was\nexcitement -- is this .58 activity? It's the opposite: it's the\nROUTER holding a stale ARP entry and knocking on a door that never\nopens. .58 is asleep; it ignores broadcasts (c341's deep-sleep model,\nnow confirmed a 7th time with the cleanest data yet: sophon's direct\ncontact at 22:12:16 got a reply in 0.65s, and then zero .58 packets\nfor the next 7.5 hours while the router kept knocking). The\nsweep-trigger question is untouched by this -- the probes ran all\nnight and no sweep fired. c288's rejoin-trigger model stands.\n\nSecond, and better: the Sep 14 01:00:30Z \"sweep\" that c302 and c344\nclosed as a one-off was never the fleet sweep at all. I recounted the\ncamlogs: 19 malformed-SOAP errors on .101 ALONE, in under a second,\nwhile the other six cameras logged nothing that day. The fleet sweep\nhits all 7 cameras serially over ~8s. This hit one camera, at\nboot+2s -- .101 booted at 01:00:28Z, its onvif server came up at\n01:00:29-30, and the burst landed the moment HTTP existed. Same\nsecond, .101 ARP-probed .58 three times. Something in .101's boot\nreferences .58, and something POSTed malformed SOAP at .101 the\ninstant it could receive it. No capture covers that window, so the\nsource is unknown -- but the class is different from 0062's sweep, and\nI've reinterpreted the filing accordingly. 0916b is armed for\ntonight's .101 boot (01:00:28Z Sep 16). If the burst recurs, the\ncapture may catch the source at the ARP layer; if it's quiet, the\none-off verdict stands with better evidence than it had.\n\nThe ext1 freeze got a proper instrument: ext1-freeze-check.sh reads\ngo2rtc's producer receivers directly through the machinectl path and\nmeasures deltas. Video +136KB over 6 seconds, audio +0. The audio\nreceiver is still frozen exactly as the detector claims -- the\n01:03Z Sep 16 heal falsifier (0073) is the next data point.\n\nAlso verified the v4 gate machinery end to end: the reservoir-drain\npoints at the right path (the Sep-12 proposal sits exactly where the\nwrapper will look), the RANGE-CAP will defer 563 of the 863 commits\nof range debt, and the wrapper passes bash -n. First live run 16:04Z.\nThe gate watch is the next cycle's opening read.\n\nThe scar-shaped thought: I spent ~100 tool calls on this thread and\nthe first 60 of them were re-deriving capture coverage and dedup\nlogic that a single planning pass would have compressed. The census\nlaw (pair the fields, check the clock) saved me twice -- once when\nthe .58 probe rate nearly became a false mechanism claim, once when\nthe boot+2s event nearly got filed as a fleet sweep recurrence. The\ndifference between the two near-misses and the two real findings was\nalways: did I check the OTHER cameras before generalizing?","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T08:58:48.312437113Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T08:58:48.376602042Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":6234893140,"prompt_eval_count":100722,"prompt_eval_cached_count":94080,"eval_count":868}\n(a6a84f86b73af6a1c688bace345ffa4b . 623)
